  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, with temperatures trending above normal \nthrough Friday morning.\n\nA high pressure system off the North Carolina coast lends \nsupport for a mid-level ridge stretching into New England, \nbringing a warmer and drier airmass to southern New England. \nWhile this conjunction will enhance the warming trend through \nFriday, allowing temperatures to climb into mid 70s today and \nnear 80 by Friday, some low-level moisture from a shortwave over\nthe Great Lakes will lead to quite a bit of cloud cover over \nthe next few days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chances for showers return Friday evening as a \ncold front sweeps through New England. \n\nThe next round of showers arrives Friday evening as a low \npressure system moves across Ontario and Quebec, and a trailing \ncold front sweeps through New England. Guidance has honed in on \nthe later evening hours, likely after sunset, and into the night\nfor the FROPA. Some CAMs are even showing the showers falling \napart as the front moves further southeast, leading to higher \nconfidence that this system will not bring significant \nrainfall... likely a quarter inch or less.\n\nOnce the low moves through Quebec, a rather strong continental \nhigh pressure system will build over the Great Lakes and \nsouthern Ontario, sinking those +4C to +6C 850mb temperatures \ndown into New England and sharply dropping surface temperatures \nthrough the weekend... overnight temps likely drop into the \nupper 30s to low 40s and daytime highs in the mid 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A secondary, sharp temperature drop is expected\nfor the start of next week.\n\nTowards the second half of the weekend and into early next \nweek, ensemble guidance continues to indicate a deepening low \nsweeps across the James Bay. There's still some uncertainty on \nif the high Saturday will be strong enough to stave off any \nprecip reaching as far south as southern New England Sunday \nnight, but the last few model runs have seemed to suggest there \ncould be a sprinkle or two... likely not anything significant, \nif showers manifest at all. \n\nA strong high pressure system then builds in right on the heels\nof the low, bringing a secondary shot of even colder air to New\nEngland. 850mb temperatures could drop to near or just below 0C\nby Tuesday morning, supporting surface highs in mid 50s with \nnighttime lows possibly falling into the 30s.",
